.TH CLRU_SET 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
clru_set \- store a value by key (a type-inferred convenience wrapper)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clrucache.h>

ccol_retval_t clru_set(name, key, val);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
A type-inferred GNU C statement expression that converts key and val
to the cache's declared KeyT and ValT, the same way a plain C
assignment does, serialises the converted values into cmap_pair values
and calls clrucache_set_full().

val is converted to ValT instead of being stored with its own
expression type, so an int literal that goes into a double-valued
cache stores the double value 5.0, not a 4-byte copy of the int's bit
pattern. The compiler must be able to convert the val and key
expressions implicitly to the cache's declared ValT and KeyT, and an
expression that it cannot convert is a compile error at this point.
A character-pointer KeyT or ValT is the exception: the macro keeps the
pointer type of the expression, so a const char * key or value for a
char * cache needs no cast.

A key of a struct type is the same key as another one only when every
byte of the two is equal, padding included; see KEYS WITH PADDING in
clrucache_create_full(3). With GCC 11 or later the macro sets the
padding of its own copy of the key to zero.

If the caller configured a remote setter at construction, it is called
synchronously before the cache is updated. If the setter fails, the
macro returns ccol_unexpected_failure and nothing changes for that
key, not even its position in the eviction order. While the setter
runs, a concurrent insert that needs room can evict the key like any
other entry. Every allocation of the macro happens before the remote
setter runs, so a remote setter that succeeds is always followed by an
update of the cache that cannot fail; see clrucache_set_full(3).
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name  Cache variable. It must be a plain variable, declared with
      clru_construct or clru_declare, or restored with
      clru_redeclare() in the current scope, because that is where
      the KeyT and the ValT for the conversion above come from. name
      cannot be any other expression, for example a read of a struct
      member.
key   Key expression: any expression that the compiler can convert
      implicitly to KeyT.
val   Value expression: any expression that the compiler can convert
      implicitly to ValT.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success             The macro stored the value, and the remote
                         setter succeeded, if there is one.
ccol_unexpected_failure  The remote setter returned false. The set
                         changed nothing, but a concurrent insert can
                         have evicted the key (see above).
ccol_invalid_args        cache is CLRU_CACHE_INVALID, or cache is a
                         stale handle that a call already destroyed, or
                         an argument is a null pointer, or an argument
                         has a size of zero. A NULL key or value of a
                         character-pointer type is not a string, so it
                         gives this code.
ccol_not_enough_memory   An allocation failed (the copy of the new
                         value, or the record of a new key). The
                         remote setter was not called, and nothing
                         changed for the key.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro uses a GNU C statement expression ({...}), so it needs GCC
or Clang.
When you pass the cache handle across scopes, for example into a
thread function, declare a local alias and call clru_redeclare() on it
before you call clru_set(); clru_get() needs the same. See
clru_redeclare(3).
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
clru_construct(cache, int, double, 128, NULL, NULL, NULL);

int k = 42;
double v = 3.14;
clru_set(cache, k, v);

clru_destroy(cache);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clru_get (3),
.BR clrucache_set_full (3)
